      <p class="ui text-base-content/70">The essay</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 display">The hour after the sun</h2>
      <div class="copy mt-10 text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <h3>A light that does not go</h3>
      <p>Anyone who has spent a June evening in Copenhagen, Stockholm or Oslo knows the moment. The sun has set, but the sky does not darken. It thins instead, from gold to a pale, cool blue that seems to come from everywhere at once, and it stays like that for hours. Shadows disappear. Colors go quiet and close together. A white dress, a white church or a white wall holds the light longer than anything around it.</p>
      <p>Between 1885 and 1915, a generation of Scandinavian painters made that hour their subject. Many had trained in Paris and admired the Impressionists, who painted the bright, broken light of noon. They came home and found a light the French had never painted: slow, even, and blue.</p>
      <h3>Six painters, three countries</h3>
      <p>At Skagen, at the northern tip of Denmark, P. S. Krøyer walked the beach after supper with his wife and their friends and painted them dissolving into the sea. Anna Ancher, born in Skagen, painted the same light coming indoors. In Copenhagen, Vilhelm Hammershøi watched it leave the rooms of his apartment. In Stockholm, Eugène Jansson painted the city from the heights of Södermalm as its lamps came on, and Richard Bergh posed two friends on a veranda at midnight in June. In Norway, Harald Sohlberg painted a mountain range by starlight and a table left out on a summer night.</p>
      <blockquote><p>They came home from Paris and found a light the French had never painted: slow, even, and blue.</p></blockquote>
      <p>They did not form a school, and some never met. But they read the same critics, showed in the same exhibitions in Copenhagen, Stockholm and Kristiania. Bergh called it the Nordic mood. It was not a style so much as an attention: to stillness, to a few colors held close, and to the hour when the day is over and the night has not begun.</p>
      <h3>Hanging the evening</h3>
      <p>Long Light brings twelve of their paintings together in five rooms. Each room is painted a color of the dusk: the putty of Skagen sand, a lichen green for Hammershøi's rooms, the deep blue of Jansson's Stockholm, the near-black of a winter night in the mountains, and white for the white nights, where the exhibition ends.</p>
      <p>The works hang on one center line, at the height of a standing visitor's eyes, so that their real sizes speak: a Sohlberg nearly two meters wide, a Hammershøi you could carry under your arm. There are no frames on these pages and none of the ornate gilding many of these paintings wear in their own museums. We wanted you to see the paint.</p>
      <p>Come at the end of the day if you can. On Thursdays the museum stays open until eight, and from the windows of the last room you can watch the light go over the Columbia, slowly, the way it does in the paintings.</p>
      </div>
